1
00:00:01,430 --> 00:00:04,400
OK, so welcome back, everyone.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:07,443
Today, we'll go
more into circuits

3
00:00:07,443 --> 00:00:09,360
as you did yesterday in
the afternoon session.

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,620
Just this time, it's going to
be circuits with extra features.

5
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:14,880
On top of them are more
specialized circuits.

6
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:17,037
So we'll start with
synaptic plasticity

7
00:00:17,037 --> 00:00:18,120
in reinforcement learning.

8
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:20,870
So in other words, we
will model circuits

9
00:00:20,870 --> 00:00:25,370
with learning rules or synaptic
plasticity rules in them that

10
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:28,400
adapt over time.

11
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:32,119
Now, the synaptic
plasticity in general

12
00:00:32,119 --> 00:00:35,670
is the adaptations that
happen within synapses.

13
00:00:35,670 --> 00:00:39,050
And as we saw yesterday
morning, synapses

14
00:00:39,050 --> 00:00:43,220
is how neurons communicate
with one another.

15
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:47,420
So when we say that one synapse
is potentiated or becomes

16
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:49,730
strengthened, then
it is more it's

17
00:00:49,730 --> 00:00:52,940
easier for a presynaptic
neuron to communicate spikes

18
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:54,740
to a postsynaptic one.

19
00:00:54,740 --> 00:00:58,580
And likewise, depression
or weakening the synapse

20
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:00,220
is the opposite of this.

21
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:03,910
And we can have different
synaptic mechanisms working

22
00:01:03,910 --> 00:01:05,800
on different timescales
within the brain,

23
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:08,905
from milliseconds to
even minutes and longer.

24
00:01:08,905 --> 00:01:15,230
And a very classic rule
about synaptic plasticity,

25
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:18,130
you probably have heard of this,
but put forth by Donald Hebb

26
00:01:18,130 --> 00:01:21,710
in the '40s about neurons that
fire together, wire together,

27
00:01:21,710 --> 00:01:25,660
which, in other words, means
that if the spike times of two

28
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:28,215
neurons are very close to one
another and these neurons are

29
00:01:28,215 --> 00:01:29,840
connected, of course,
with the synapse,

30
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:32,440
then the synapse would
get strengthened.

31
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:34,870
And we will see essentially
different flavors

32
00:01:34,870 --> 00:01:41,170
of this Hebbian rule
today in the exercises.

33
00:01:41,170 --> 00:01:46,660
So you have seen this
the corticostriatal model

34
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:50,620
in the first day's talk,
and partial view of that you

35
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:54,480
have built yesterday.

36
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:56,230
And essentially, this
model was originally

37
00:01:56,230 --> 00:01:57,920
designed to do
category learning.

38
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:00,770
And as you might remember, the
task with category learning

39
00:02:00,770 --> 00:02:05,900
was originating in the macaque
literature, where, essentially,

40
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:11,630
the monkey had to decide whether
a specific dot pattern, so

41
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:16,020
a cue like this one, belongs to
category left or category right.

42
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:20,930
And these are the two prototypes
where that essentially

43
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:22,710
generated all the stimuli.

44
00:02:22,710 --> 00:02:25,380
Of course, the monkey had
never seen the prototypes,

45
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:28,580
but the challenge was to come up
with a categorization rule that

46
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:31,170
can distinguish
between these things.

47
00:02:31,170 --> 00:02:34,200
So every stimulus here, I
should say, is an exemplar.

48
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:36,980
And this is a distorted image
of either one of the two

49
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:37,530
prototypes.

50
00:02:37,530 --> 00:02:42,800
So the animals never see
exactly the prototype.

51
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:47,270
So, yeah, the animal has
never seen the prototype.

52
00:02:47,270 --> 00:02:50,990
Just by trial and error and
taking actions and receiving

53
00:02:50,990 --> 00:02:54,200
feedback, it has to learn how
to distinguish between the two

54
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:54,750
categories.

55
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:58,470
And this, we claim,
is essentially

56
00:02:58,470 --> 00:03:01,090
driven by synaptic plasticity
in the animal's brain.

57
00:03:01,090 --> 00:03:05,565
And this is what the full
corticostriatal model building

58
00:03:05,565 --> 00:03:08,525
neuron blocks has in it as well.

59
00:03:08,525 --> 00:03:10,650
So here, I'm just showing
you the model again, just

60
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:13,830
to remind you of the
structure and also highlight

61
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:16,616
a few components which
have to do with the task.

62
00:03:16,616 --> 00:03:19,250
So the stimulus coming
into the model, this

63
00:03:19,250 --> 00:03:20,250
is [INAUDIBLE] stimulus.

64
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:22,420
You build [INAUDIBLE]
stimuli yesterday.

65
00:03:22,420 --> 00:03:25,720
So these are the dot patterns
coming in to visual cortex.

66
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:29,280
And after the cortex to
the striatum connections

67
00:03:29,280 --> 00:03:31,330
and to other
subcortical regions,

68
00:03:31,330 --> 00:03:33,900
we have the final action,
which is the saccade

69
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:36,540
because the monkey chooses
left or right by looking

70
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:38,430
at the left or right direction.

71
00:03:38,430 --> 00:03:42,220
And after the action, we also
have the behavioral feedback,

72
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:44,520
which is reward or
no reward according

73
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:47,520
to the other choice made.

74
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:51,790
So these are all part of the
model, so within your block's

75
00:03:51,790 --> 00:03:55,620
model actual behavioral
tasks, and link them

76
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:59,260
to a circuit model
with no problem.

77
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:01,880
And as I mentioned
yesterday as well,

78
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:05,140
this model was
built on principles

79
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:09,410
about structure and function
that we have about the brain.

80
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:12,550
And it was constrained
by specific parameters

81
00:04:12,550 --> 00:04:14,750
that we got from the literature.

82
00:04:14,750 --> 00:04:17,839
It was never directly fitted
to data from this task.

83
00:04:17,839 --> 00:04:21,003
Yet it can match both the
physiological elements

84
00:04:21,003 --> 00:04:23,170
of the task, as you can see
here, in the [INAUDIBLE]

85
00:04:23,170 --> 00:04:25,160
LFB and its spectrogram.

86
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:28,425
And also, it can match the
learning behavior over time.

87
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:36,040
And it can actually match
both the [INAUDIBLE]

88
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:39,190
phase and the behavior
because of the learning rules

89
00:04:39,190 --> 00:04:41,810
that help it adapt over trials.

90
00:04:41,810 --> 00:04:45,520
And this model includes both
LTP, Long-Term Potentiation,

91
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:48,250
in the corticocortical
synapses, that

92
00:04:48,250 --> 00:04:50,920
is, the synapses between the
visual cortex and the anterior

93
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:51,940
cortex.

94
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:58,020
And it also includes
corticostriatal synapses

95
00:04:58,020 --> 00:05:00,430
that can do both LTP or LTD.

96
00:05:00,430 --> 00:05:05,760
And these are also modulated by
dopamine between the anterior

97
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:08,340
cortex and the striatum here.

98
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:11,200
And we'll get into more
detail about these.

99
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:15,850
And as a note in your blogs, we
call this units synaptic rules,

100
00:05:15,850 --> 00:05:17,400
learning rules
because this is all

101
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:20,700
in the context of
decision-making.

102
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:23,160
So the learning rule is
essentially the gradient

103
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:28,050
that the synaptic weights
follow to adapt over time.

104
00:05:28,050 --> 00:05:32,670
So first with the
corticocortical LTP,

105
00:05:32,670 --> 00:05:36,840
this is a form of Hebbian
learning, as you can see here

106
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:38,700
with this term, the LTP.

107
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:40,500
The S's here are
the activities of

108
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:42,580
the presynaptic and
postsynaptic neuron.

109
00:05:42,580 --> 00:05:45,280
And it also includes
an extra reversal term,

110
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:48,390
which essentially means
that this term is actually

111
00:05:48,390 --> 00:05:51,270
dependent on dopamine
released in VTA.

112
00:05:51,270 --> 00:05:54,600
So what's happening here
is that, during the trial,

113
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:57,680
there is potentiation
happening in the synapses,

114
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:01,480
in the corticocortical
synapses, but in anticipation

115
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:03,140
for the result, for the reward.

116
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:07,730
But maybe if the choice that
the animal makes is wrong,

117
00:06:07,730 --> 00:06:09,670
then there is
actually a reversal

118
00:06:09,670 --> 00:06:11,650
of that potentiation
mediated by this term

119
00:06:11,650 --> 00:06:15,410
here, which happens
through dopamine VTA

120
00:06:15,410 --> 00:06:18,160
because whether there the
animal receives a reward

121
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:21,490
or not actually drives the
dopamine release in VTA, which,

122
00:06:21,490 --> 00:06:24,075
in turn, drives this
reversal or no reversal.

123
00:06:27,295 --> 00:06:30,010
So here, you can see
an example of how

124
00:06:30,010 --> 00:06:34,880
this plasticity rule drives
the weight changes over trials.

125
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:38,840
So here, we have simulated
700 trials of the task.

126
00:06:38,840 --> 00:06:42,940
Each trial is a separate
stimulus and one action and one

127
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:44,635
reward feedback.

128
00:06:44,635 --> 00:06:46,600
And as you can see, this
is an adjacency matrix

129
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:49,017
for the weights between the
visual cortex and the anterior

130
00:06:49,017 --> 00:06:49,670
cortex.

131
00:06:49,670 --> 00:06:53,260
And you can see the weight value
is changing from the beginning

132
00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:54,650
to the end of the task.

133
00:06:54,650 --> 00:06:57,330
And likewise, you can
see the average weight

134
00:06:57,330 --> 00:07:00,660
in these same synapses
actually adapting over time

135
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:04,320
and reaching a stable value.

136
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:08,430
So moving on to the second
place, where we have implemented

137
00:07:08,430 --> 00:07:10,080
synaptic plasticity
in this model, that

138
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:14,490
is the connections from the
anterior cortex to the striatum,

139
00:07:14,490 --> 00:07:19,830
so these connections follow a
more sophisticated rule which

140
00:07:19,830 --> 00:07:22,980
can induce both LTP and LTD.

141
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:25,170
And essentially,
this rule, again,

142
00:07:25,170 --> 00:07:27,850
the changes in weight,
the weight gradient.

143
00:07:27,850 --> 00:07:32,430
In other words, it has the
typical Hebbian-like component

144
00:07:32,430 --> 00:07:34,380
where it is a function
of the activities

145
00:07:34,380 --> 00:07:36,310
of the presynaptic and
postsynaptic neuron.

146
00:07:36,310 --> 00:07:39,480
But now, this
Hebbian component is

147
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:43,710
modulated by the DA,
the dopamine, released

148
00:07:43,710 --> 00:07:45,990
in the substantia nigra.

149
00:07:45,990 --> 00:07:48,480
So essentially, here,
we have a third region,

150
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:51,840
this S and C working as a
modulator in the connections

151
00:07:51,840 --> 00:07:55,300
between cortex and striatum.

152
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:57,430
And there is also
a third term, which

153
00:07:57,430 --> 00:08:00,930
actually depends on the current
values of these weights.

154
00:08:03,700 --> 00:08:07,990
And as I was saying before,
because of this immediate tie

155
00:08:07,990 --> 00:08:11,080
to dopamine, any reward
feedback can actually

156
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:15,460
either strengthen the
synapses or punishment

157
00:08:15,460 --> 00:08:18,400
or omission of reward can
also weaken the synapses

158
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:19,485
and induce LTP.

159
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:24,850
And also, this rule,
you can work out

160
00:08:24,850 --> 00:08:27,760
the math, which actually you can
see in more detail in this paper

161
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:29,320
as well.

162
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:34,299
If you expand on the
dopamine term, which

163
00:08:34,299 --> 00:08:37,510
is essentially dopamine
released in S and C,

164
00:08:37,510 --> 00:08:40,460
it has some baseline dopamine
plus this epsilon value.

165
00:08:40,460 --> 00:08:42,909
And this epsilon
value is, essentially,

166
00:08:42,909 --> 00:08:45,130
the difference between
the actual reward

167
00:08:45,130 --> 00:08:49,170
received and the expected value
of the reward to be received.

168
00:08:49,170 --> 00:08:52,970
And this difference shows
up in classic reinforcement

169
00:08:52,970 --> 00:08:55,730
learning tools like Q-learning.

170
00:08:55,730 --> 00:08:57,780
Or even in temporal
difference learning as well,

171
00:08:57,780 --> 00:09:01,070
you can see a flavor of
this plus some extra terms.

172
00:09:01,070 --> 00:09:03,260
So this is a nice result
that highlights the fact

173
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:06,590
that simpler agent
models can actually

174
00:09:06,590 --> 00:09:11,940
relate to neurobiology through
this synaptic plasticity rule.

175
00:09:11,940 --> 00:09:13,670
And here you can
see a result, which

176
00:09:13,670 --> 00:09:17,590
is a hyperparameter search over
having two different parameter

177
00:09:17,590 --> 00:09:18,090
sets.

178
00:09:18,090 --> 00:09:21,720
You can see, in fact, how
varying this, essentially,

179
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:24,350
this prediction error, which
is the epsilon, the difference

180
00:09:24,350 --> 00:09:26,580
between actual value
and predicted value.

181
00:09:26,580 --> 00:09:31,430
How does this affect the weight
gradient, the delta of the W

182
00:09:31,430 --> 00:09:32,960
here?

183
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:38,390
And how this can reduce both
depression and potentiation

184
00:09:38,390 --> 00:09:41,480
according-- based on the
science, essentially,

185
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:44,375
of the prediction error.

186
00:09:44,375 --> 00:09:47,220
So what we will
build here is, again,

187
00:09:47,220 --> 00:09:51,360
a partial view of the
full model in two places.

188
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:54,970
Initially, we'll work on a
model like the second model

189
00:09:54,970 --> 00:09:57,760
from yesterday, so two cortical
regions, a [INAUDIBLE] stimulus

190
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:01,070
and an ascending system that
modulates their activity.

191
00:10:01,070 --> 00:10:03,550
And we will see how
Hebbian plasticity

192
00:10:03,550 --> 00:10:05,060
works with no feedback.

193
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:07,540
So we will remove the LTP.

194
00:10:07,540 --> 00:10:10,630
There's no LTP reversal
because we'll just

195
00:10:10,630 --> 00:10:13,940
let the system simulate
and see how it adapts.

196
00:10:13,940 --> 00:10:19,180
And in the second example, we
will include a striatum elements

197
00:10:19,180 --> 00:10:19,850
as well.

198
00:10:19,850 --> 00:10:22,450
And in this case, I will
do proper reinforcement

199
00:10:22,450 --> 00:10:24,740
learning with--

200
00:10:24,740 --> 00:10:26,330
18 months, we'll
have a category.

201
00:10:26,330 --> 00:10:30,140
There are correct-labeled
categories.

202
00:10:30,140 --> 00:10:32,990
And the model will have to
make a decision on each trial.

203
00:10:32,990 --> 00:10:36,280
And we will judge whether
the decision is good or bad

204
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:38,270
and then adapt the
weights accordingly.

205
00:10:38,270 --> 00:10:40,240
And you will see how this works.

206
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:42,340
So, yeah, this was all.

207
00:10:42,340 --> 00:10:45,880
So you can navigate to
the synaptic plasticity

208
00:10:45,880 --> 00:10:48,110
and reinforcement
learning notebook.

209
00:10:48,110 --> 00:10:51,500
And again, feel free to
ask us any questions.

